Standard sand core specimens (typically 50 mm x 50 mm) prepared with the binder must satisfy minimum physical strength parameters:
a) Early green compression strength (e.g. 0.010 MPa within 15 minutes to prevent core collapse during handling);
b) In-process scratch hardness or mould strength (measured on side walls immediately after stripping);
c) Sustained final compression strength after complete room-temperature curing (attaining a minimum of 1.72 MPa or 17.5 kg/cm² after 240 minutes).

To account for deflection, bending, and warping during hot metal forming, the standard increases both machining allowances and dimensional tolerances for slender forgings based on the ratio of length (b) to section size (a):
a) For b/a <= 7: Normal working allowances and tolerances apply as per Tables 1, 2, and 3;
b) For b/a <= 8: Increase size-specific allowances and tolerances by 15 percent;
c) For b/a <= 9: Increase size-specific allowances and tolerances by 30 percent;
d) For b/a <= 10: Increase size-specific allowances and tolerances by 75 percent;
e) For b/a > 10: Specific allowances and tolerances must be mutually agreed between the manufacturer and the purchaser on an individual basis.

Tubes are supplied as specified by the purchaser in one of three conditions:
a) O — Annealed (soft) [supplied in coils or straight lengths];
b) HB — Half-hard [supplied in straight lengths];
c) HD — Hard [supplied in straight lengths].

Tubes are designated by outside diameter and wall thickness, classified across four functional tables:
a) Table W (Micro Bore Annealed): sizes 6, 8, 10 mm OD (thickness 0.6 mm preferred, or 0.8 mm recommended). Max working pressure: 6.2 to 9.0 MPa (preferred) and 8.2 to 14.4 MPa (recommended);
b) Table X (Half-Hard): sizes 6 to 159 mm OD (thickness 0.6 mm to 2.0 mm). Max working pressure: 1.5 to 13.3 MPa;
c) Table Y (Half-Hard and Annealed): sizes 6 to 108 mm OD (thickness 0.8 mm to 2.5 mm). Max working pressure: 2.9 to 18.8 MPa (half-hard) and 2.3 to 14.4 MPa (annealed);
d) Table Z (Hard Drawn): sizes 6 to 159 mm OD (thickness 0.5 mm to 1.5 mm). Max working pressure: 1.5 to 11.3 MPa.

Tubes must satisfy minimum mechanical limits tested in full section or longitudinal strips per IS 1608:
- Annealed (O) [up to 108 mm size]: Tensile strength (Min) = 210 MPa, Elongation (Min) = 40%;
- Half-hard (HB) [up to 159 mm size]: Tensile strength (Min) = 250 MPa, Elongation (Min) = 30%;
- Hard (HD) [up to 54 mm size]: Tensile strength (Min) = 280 MPa;
- Hard (HD) [54 to 159 mm size]: Tensile strength (Min) = 310 MPa.

For tubes designated in Tables W, X, and Y (O and HB tempers), a 45-degree taper drift is plunged into the unannealed tube end per IS 2335. The tube must expand without cracking or showing physical flow:
- Table W (O condition): minimum 30% expansion;
- Table X (HB up to 54 mm): minimum 35% expansion;
- Table X (HB 54 to 108 mm): minimum 25% expansion;
- Table X (HB 108 to 159 mm): minimum 15% expansion;
- Table Y (O condition up to 108 mm): minimum 30% expansion;
- Table Y (HB up to 35 mm): minimum 30% expansion;
- Table Y (HB 35 to 108 mm): minimum 30% expansion;
- Table Z (HD condition): Drift expansion is not applicable.

To ensure reliable leak-proof compression fittings, strict geometric limits are enforced:
- Mean Outside Diameter: half the sum of two diameters measured at right angles;
- Mean Wall Thickness: half the sum of two diametrically opposite wall thicknesses;
- Ovality: calculated as (OD_max - OD_min) / OD_avg;
- Eccentricity: calculated as (WT_max - WT_min) / WT_avg.

Tubes must be manufactured from one of seven specified grades of copper or copper alloy designed for particular thermal, mechanical, and corrosion-resistance conditions:
a) Cu-DLP: Phosphorized Low Residual Phosphorus Copper (Purity: >=99.90% min copper, P: 0.004-0.012%);
b) Cu-DHP: Phosphorized High Residual Phosphorus Copper (Purity: >=99.90% min copper, P: 0.015-0.040%);
b) CuZn30As: 70/30 Brass (Arsenical, for bio-fouling and dezincification resistance);
d) CuZn29Sn1As: Admiralty Brass (Arsenical, typical for freshwater/saline condensers);
e) CuZn21Al2As: Aluminium Brass (Arsenical, typical for high-velocity cooling water);
f) CuNi10Fe1: Cupronickel 90/10 (corrosion and erosion resistant);
g) CuNi30Mn1Fe: Cupronickel 70/30 (highly resistant to seawater and high-temperature steam).

Tubes must be supplied in one of five distinct physical conditions as specified by the purchaser:
a) As Drawn (HD): Full hard condition produced by cold drawing, which can be stress-relieved;
b) As Drawn and End Annealed: Full hard condition but with tube ends fully annealed to prevent cracking during expansion into tube-sheets;
c) Half Hard (HB): Produced by cold drawing to an intermediate hardness;
d) Temper Annealed (TA): Heat-treated over their whole length to an intermediate condition;
e) Annealed (O): Fully annealed for maximum ductility and malleability.

Strict geometric definitions are established to ensure leak-tight fittings and uniform flow:
a) Eccentricity: calculated as (t_max - t_min) / t_avg where t is the wall thickness;
b) Ovality: calculated as (OD_max - OD_min) / OD_avg where OD is the outside diameter;
c) Out of Roundness: defined as the absolute difference (OD_max - OD_min).

The strip must satisfy mechanical limits based on thickness and temper:
- Annealed (O) [0.15 to 1.8mm]: Tensile Strength = 205-250 MPa, Elongation (Min) = 30%, Hardness (Max) = 60 HV;
- Annealed (O) [1.8 to 6.0mm]: Tensile Strength = 205-250 MPa, Elongation (Min) = 35%, Hardness (Max) = 60 HV;
- Half-hard (HB) [0.15 to 1.8mm]: Tensile Strength = 245 MPa Min, Elongation (Min) = 10%, Hardness = 75-90 HV;
- Half-hard (HB) [1.8 to 6.0mm]: Tensile Strength = 245 MPa Min, Elongation (Min) = 15%, Hardness = 75-90 HV;
- Hard (HD) [0.15 to 6.0mm]: Tensile Strength = 290 MPa Min, Hardness = 90 HV Min.

Both transverse and longitudinal specimens must withstand close bending around a specified former without showing cracks or fractures on the outer convex surface:
- Annealed (O) and Half-hard (HB): 180-degree angle, close-contact (flat) radius;
- Hard (HD): 90-degree angle, close-contact radius.

If requested, the strip must meet proof stress controls to qualify as hard/half-hard, categorized into designations:
- CPR1: Thickness 0.80 to 6.0 mm, 0.1% Proof Stress = 140-200 MPa;
- CPR2: Thickness 0.80 to 1.20 mm, 0.1% Proof Stress = 170-230 MPa;
- CPR2: Thickness 1.20 to 6.0 mm, 0.1% Proof Stress = 170-220 MPa;
- CPR3: Thickness 0.80 to 6.0 mm, 0.1% Proof Stress = 220-260 MPa.

Strips must feature either square or radiused corners. If radiused, the nominal corner radius must comply with standard ranges based on strip thickness:
- Thickness up to 1.0 mm: Semicircular radius (Tolerance: ±0.06 mm);
- Thickness 1.0 to 1.6 mm: 0.6 mm radius (Tolerance: +0.15 mm, -0.10 mm);
- Thickness 1.6 to 2.25 mm: 0.8 mm radius (Tolerance: ±0.15 mm);
- Thickness 2.25 to 3.55 mm: 1.0 mm radius (Tolerance: ±0.20 mm);
- Thickness 3.55 mm and over: 1.25 mm radius (Tolerance: ±0.25 mm).

Rods and bars are manufactured from high-purity Phosphorous Deoxidized non-arsenical Copper (equivalent to Grade Cu-DHP or BS 2874 C106), demonstrating a minimum copper content of 99.90 percent (Cu + Ag >= 99.90% min). Highly restricted element limits are enforced to prevent cracking during hot fabrication:
a) Phosphorus (P): 0.013 to 0.050 percent (commonly 0.015 to 0.050%);
b) Arsenic (As): 0.05 percent Max (or 0.06% Max);
c) Lead (Pb): 0.01 percent Max (or 0.03% Max);
d) Iron (Fe): 0.03 percent Max (or 0.05% Max);
e) Other total impurities: 0.06 percent Max.

Rods are tested in both the 'As manufactured' (M) and 'Annealed' (O) conditions. Mechanical properties must satisfy specified ranges depending on the specific diameter/width across flats:
a) Tensile Strength typically ranges between 130 to 270 MPa (or 13 to 27.5 kg/mm²);
b) Elongation at break must be at least 40 percent in the Annealed (O) state.

Wired specimens must satisfy mechanical limits based on condition:
a) Annealed (O) condition: High ductility and flexibility, with an elongation at break of at least 35 to 40 percent;
b) Hard-drawn (HD) condition: High tensile strength to withstand high-speed spooling and structural mechanical loading.

For quality audit inspections under Scheme-I, random samples must be drawn as per Table 1 (Levels of Control):
- Physical testing: 6-meter sample length;
- Chemical analysis: 5 pieces of 5 cm length.
